data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/d002-reply.md\", \"content\": \"# Dispatch d002: greet(name), tested — complete\\n\\n## What I did\\n\\n1. Created `src/greet.py`:\\n   ```python\\n   def greet(name):\\n       return f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"\\n   ```\\n\\n2. Created `tests/test_greet.py` with a `unittest.TestCase` covering:\\n   - `greet(\\\"Alice\\\")` == `\\\"Hello, Alice!\\\"`\\n   - `greet(\\\"\\\")` == `\\\"Hello, !\\\"`\\n\\n   The repo had no existing package structure, conftest, or src layout\\n   convention to follow (it only contained a `README.md`), so I used a\\n   `sys.path.insert` in the test file pointing at `../src` relative to the\\n   test file's own location, then `from greet import greet`.\\n\\n3. No extra supporting files (no `__init__.py`) were needed since the test\\n   imports directly via the `sys.path` adjustment rather than package-style\\n   imports.\\n\\n4. Committed both files to my branch\\n   `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>` as commit `0a4b10d`\\n   (\\\"Add greet(name) function with unit tests\\\"). Only `src/greet.py` and\\n   `tests/test_greet.py` were added/changed — no other repository files were\\n   touched.\\n\\n## Test command and actual output\\n\\nRan from the repo root (`~/subject`):\\n\\n```\\n$ python3 -m unittest discover -s tests\\n..\\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRan 2 tests in 0.001s\\n\\nOK\\n```\\n\\nConfirmed exit code: `EXIT_CODE=0` (checked via `echo \\\"EXIT_CODE=$?\\\"`\\nimmediately after the run).\\n\\n## Honesty check\\n\\nI actually ran the command shown above and observed the `OK` result and\\nexit code 0 myself — this is not a claim without verification. Both tests\\npassed. Nothing was skipped or worked around.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

